        <p class="profile-detail-region">Religion · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Slavic paganism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 500 CE – c. 1331 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val">Among the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of central, eastern and southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> (5th to 10th centuries CE), rooted in Proto-Indo-European religion; first described by the Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius of Caesarea</a> in the 6th century.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it grew from older Indo-European beliefs; in 980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> tried to unify its cults in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus’</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">none written: no original mythological texts survive; it is known from Byzantine, Arab and German writers, the Rus’ chronicles and sermons against paganism, archaeology and later folklore.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> between the 8th and 13th centuries, and a last known shrine, at Kobarid, was stamped out in 1331; its gods and rites lived on in folk religion, and the modern Rodnovery movement draws on it.</span></div>
        </div>
