    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> worshipped a supreme god of the sky and thunder, later known as Perun, alongside Veles of the underworld, Mokosh, Dazhbog and a host of spirits of waters, forests, fields, households and ancestors.</li><li>West Slavic temples held many-headed idols, such as the four-headed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svetovid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> and the three-headed Triglav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a>, kept by priests called volkhvs.</li><li>Rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> (863), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> (864), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> (966) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus’</a> (988), but popular resistance lasted for centuries, and the old rites lived on in a “double faith”.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to legend, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> sought a new faith for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus’</a>, his envoys found “no joy … but sorrow and great stench” in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, but were won over by the beauty of the rites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

